\mt1 EDITOR’S INTRODUCTION
\ip This volume brings together two English translations that have long been read apart. The Old Testament is the Septuagint in the translation of Sir Lancelot Charles Lee Brenton, as printed in 1870 and as published online by eBible.org with its corrections. Brenton’s own preface, introductions, notes, and appendices are kept. The New Testament is the Authorized, or King James, Version in the text of the Cambridge Paragraph Bible, likewise as published online by eBible.org, together with the Epistle Dedicatory and The Translators to the Reader. Scrivener’s critical introduction and appendices to the Cambridge edition were never part of the online text and do not appear here. Both publishers describe their texts as public domain.
\ip Each testament opens with the front matter of its own translation: Brenton’s preface, introductions, and list of abbreviations come before Genesis, and the Epistle Dedicatory and The Translators to the Reader before Matthew. Brenton’s appendices and eBible’s corrections follow Revelation.
\ip The books of the Old Testament are arranged in the order of the Church of Greece. So the Prayer of Manasses follows 2 Chronicles, 4 Maccabees follows 3 Maccabees, and the additions to Esther and Daniel and Psalm 151 stand within the text rather than apart from it. Brenton’s single book of Ezra and Nehemiah is divided into 2 Esdras and Nehemias, and Malachias 3:19–24 is numbered 4:1–6, again after the Greek usage. A few books are renamed in the same spirit: Brenton’s Joshua is Jesus, the Son of Navi, and his four books of Kings are the four books of Kingdoms. Every book is then given a full title in the manner of the Cambridge Bible, whose Genesis is “The First Book of Moses, Called Genesis,” and a short name for the running heads.
\ip The wording of both translations, and of their notes, has not been altered. Words that the translators supplied to complete the English sense, which many Bibles print in italics, are set here in ordinary type. The chapter and verse numbers are otherwise those of the translations themselves. The title pages, the section dividers, the table of contents, and the headings are this edition’s own, and the opinions expressed in the introductions and appendices are those of their original authors.
\ip The volume was typeset in Utopia with PTXprint, using the page design it provides for the Berean Standard Bible at A5 size.
